 Canal was opened because the  Caravan routes going through Aleppo died  and all the merchants went into the  Lebanon or or or to Egypt and I think  that we have an obligation to deal with  displaced people not just because we  don't have the choice of ignoring it you  can't have millions of people spreading  through a society not being looked after  without terrible implications for  society but I think it's a moral  obligation we have to help those in need  and one of the efforts are making is to  create at central level at the in the EU  a billion dollar fund to pay out on  social impact bonds which are going to  fund organizations that can help the  absorption of immigrants I mean another  body that you've created is a Portland  trust which seeks to encourage private  sector in both Israel and Palestine as a  means to peace in the Middle East yeah  can you talk us through how business can  or should help resolve conflict  but there are two people in the audience  of fear Samson and Travis Hollingsworth  affair worked with me in Israel at the  Portland trust and Travis worked with me  at big society capital and I think you  know they are examples of people who  feel that one can make a difference by  following these approaches and that you  can do good and do well at the same time  the innovation that is coming now is  coming because there are very powerful  drivers as I have said governments on  the one hand don't have the money a  millennial generation of which you're a  part that feels it wants to do more than  just make money and investors who to the  tune of fifty five trillion dollars have  signed up to the United Nations  principles of responsible investment  saying we owe it to generate more than  just financial returns and social sector  organizations not-for-profits that say  to themselves we want to grow if we're  helping 700 homeless people in the UK  we'd like to help 7,000 there are 30,000  or 50,000 homeless people at any given  time in in the UK these are powerful  drivers and I think the fundamental  change that has come is to say the  allocation can't be on the allocation of  resources in financial terms in terms of  the human capital that you all represent  here today cannot come just on the basis  of risk and return I think being a  business that's in it for just money is  not going to be a popular business  already if you want to attract the  greatest talent in places like McKinsey  and elsewhere you have to elevate the  goals you have to give the opportunity  to entrance to do things that are going  to  proved society and so the transformation  that is going to come through business  is going to start as the tech revolution  did is going to start with the  disruptors who are young and who say I'm  going to create a business which is  going to deliver the best combination of  risk return and impact and I'm going to  attract the most talented people because  it's a more inspiring ago and it's it'll  pull behind their justice tected the big  corporates some of which are reacting  today I was at the launch of a hundred  and twenty-five million euro impact fund  launched by the Mars Corporation and the  Danone Food Corporation in France two  huge food corporations to take a million  people in their supply chain out of  poverty Unilever is beginning to publish  a set of social objectives for itself as  a company and big companies will follow  if you look ahead twenty years  I know Einstein said the future is  unknowable but it's not unthinkable so  let's think as we think together look  ahead twenty years we can envisage  impact public equities impact fixed  income impact venture capital impact  private equity impact real estate and  impact absolute return where will the  money go if investors then have the  choice between a company that delivers a  twenty percent IRR as an investment and  another company that delivers a twenty  percent IRR and a 35 percent social  return measured because governments  publish as the UK is already doing the  cost of social issues on the web you go  to the Cabinet Office website you will  find the cost of six hundred and forty  social issues you'll be able to work out  that the young prisoner I was talking  about is costing the government  twenty-two  thousand pounds a year and if you  rehabilitate a thousand it's 22 million  and 22 million on a five million social  impact bond over seven years is more  than 35 percent rro or whatever where do  you think the money is going to go is it  going to go to those who are just  building a business to make money or is  it going to go to those who are able to  deliver a social return or an  environmental return at the same at the  same time so that's why we called our  report of the g8 task force the  invisible heart of markets because the  invisible heart of markets is the  measurement of impact and it will guide  their invisible hand and there's a  wonderful historic historic element and  it is that Adam Smith was prouder of his  book the Theory of Moral Sentiments  which is all about why people helped out  of empathy than he was out of the wealth  of nations and if he'd been around he'd  have coined the phrase the invisible  heart of markets wonderful so on the  note of thinking the future and getting  our collective minds together we'll turn  to the audience for any questions that  you may have so Gracie and Mario will be  roaming with the mic so you can just put  up your hand and and they'll come to I  think Charlie's got one of the back  there straight off the bat then the mic  will come to you in one second  thank you very much for the words I did  my undergrad at Exeter College as well  and so benefits are real your support  there so thank you for that too  my question was was about your political  views and I guess based on your your  close link to the Labour Party in the UK  whether the advancement of impact  investing had changed those views a in  terms of of what it means for the role  of the government and B with how  receptive different political parties  are to it great to meet somebody who's  an exxorian  as I am I until the Brits just amongst  you  I got a lot to Exeter College you can  imagine what it's like seven years after  coming to Britain as a refugee to walk  into the quad of a college founded in  1314 with illustrious people who'd gone  there before was a real privilege well  I start when I was a student at Oxford  where I was very involved in politics I  was president of the Oxford Union which  is the debating society as a liberal I  was a liberal for 30 years the Liberals  were in the center between the Labour  Party and the Conservative Party when  Tony Blair came in I became a Labour  Party supporter because he adopted the  policies that I believed them and when  David Cameron came in the the Cameron  government called me and said will you  come and lead the social investment and  another social investment task force  will you come to set up the social  investment bank that you recommended in  2000 and if you do and you can put  together an intelligent plan for a  social investment bank we will allow you  to take all of the unclaimed assets you  asked for we had focused in 2005 on  asking the government who was then  trying to take away bank accounts that  had been separated from their owners for  15 years or more from the banks the  banks have hold onto this money and the  Labour government had said to me we'll  give you 75 million pounds rather than  the 250 million pounds you are asking  for as equity for this bank and we said  well we'll start with a couple of  million pounds of philanthropic money  while you think about it because 75  million pounds ain't a lot of money in  financial terms when you're dealing with  the social issues we're talking about to  cut a long story short the Conservative  government allowed us to start the first  social investment bank independent of  government with just one government  representative at the trust level which  - keeping it on mission with the six  hundred million pounds a billion dollars  of which four hundred million pounds  came from unclaimed assets and I found  the Conservative government was  completely sold on the idea of the Big  Society which was basically saying you  have the government it can't cope you  need the society to do it only we didn't  know how society could do it and the  social investment bank was the first  tangible expression of what the big  society program really was so then I  began to work very closely with the  Conservative government and I like to  say that I'm about policy rather than  politics and this issue of impact  investment whether it's in the United  States where the to social impact bonds  were launched in a democratic state and  the Republican state or whether it's in  the UK we've managed to keep it as a  bipartisan issue and to stress for those  who like market forces and for those who  on the other hand feel that the state  should be responsible for all of these  things that this does both it improves  government and it brings  entrepreneurship and capital and market  forces to tackling social issues since  dave is a Bray's news chat from Oxford  as am i I'm happy you gave him a shout  out and next question okay hi my name is  Mario Mexican how do you think about the  different the line that divides what is  impact investing and what it's not to me  you know any business that makes profit  is creating jobs is creating innovation  and advancing society yeah many things  could be argued against tobacco alcohol  it would not be yeah like how do you  think about that line so there's a big  distinction we made in the g8 task force  report between investment with impact  and impact investment we're all in favor  of investment with impact businesses  that create jobs improve lives in their  communities but that's not going to deal  with social issues in the way we need  impact investment is investment where  you have measurable social or  environmental goals set and you measure  their achievement alongside financial  return and that is going to create the  innovation that will lead to a  revolution and so we see impact  investment sitting between philanthropy  on the right hand side which will begin  to measure much more as it gives grants  but will see its own business model  disrupted I will talk about that in a  moment and what we might call ESG  environmental social government  governance on on the left-hand side or  socially responsible investment or CSR  we're basically businesses intend to do  good but don't set measurable objectives  and measure their treatment intention is  not enough we're living in a time when  intention has evolved and the intention  just to make money is no longer  sufficient you need to do more than that  but the revolution is coming by bringing  the means that didn't exist before to  tackle social issues and so I think what  we're going to find over time is that  those businesses in the middle which are  businesses that set social and  environmental as well as financial  objectives and those not-for-profits  that innovate by creating measurable  goals through social impact bonds and in  other ways will bring the innovation  required to improve people's lives and  that will begin to affect the businesses  and the philanthropists on the other  side one of the great parallels for me  who lived through the tech revolution  and Silicon Valley has scored most of  the goals but we invested in the company  in that revolution that cloned Dolly the  sheep in the UK it's one of the only  companies out of Europe that made it to  the hundred most important companies of  the 20th century we lost all our money  but it was a great it was a great thing  to do it's bringing a revolution in in  medicine I think we're going to find  people now who say I want to be the  Microsoft of improving education or I  want to be the apple of improving health  and I'm going to innovate in in the way  that that I do it the disruption that is  happening is right across all the  constituencies of our of our world  so when Jeff Raikes who was CEO of the  Gates Foundation says the business model  of philanthropy is being disrupted what  does he mean he means in the future  philanthropies are going to measure  results for their grant program they're  going to make commitments to outcomes  funds that pay out on social impact  bonds and development impact bonds when  the outcomes are reached and they're  going to invest from their endowment a  certain percentage we're asking for 10%  now in investment opportunities that  have the right balance of risk return  and impact you look at the disruption to  the business model of entrepreneurs  businesses are going to raise capital on  the basis of achieving financial and  social environmental goals you look at  the disruption at the level of social  sector organizations they're going to be  able to build a balance sheet we already  see six not-for-profit service delivery  providers in the UK with sales of more  than 50 million pounds I don't know if  you saw the figures in the Dan pilota  TED talk that in 25 years in the States  50,000 businesses  made it through 50 million of sales and  only 144 not-for-profits so you have  disruption coming to social sector  organizations that can scale and fund  innovation and you have disruption for  governments which begin to measure  outcomes instead of just paying for  inputs and which attract capital to fund  innovation from investors we take the  risk of failure because they're  motivated by a blended financial and  social return so for me this impact  revolution is going to be as pervasive  as the tech revolution has been and it's  going to be led by the likes of you  you're the ones who are going to  innovate and you're the ones who are  going to scale this in such a way that  we do a better job of improving people's  lives than we've been able to do until  now so I think some of our professors  like Matt Banach who teaches here and  Heidi and Charles will be quite  delighted with the fact they've just  reinforced all the teachings they've  they've been trying to force enough over  the past couple of weeks  what matt has been there has been my  partner one of my partner's very close  partners in the task force and now in  the global steering group of 13  countries plus the EU which had  succeeded the task force so Sir Ronald I  I get the privilege of asking the last  question and as you alluded to earlier  you used to be the president of the  Oxford Union so back in the day you're  in my seat inviting the thought leaders  to share their thoughts such as Bobby  Kennedy I was just wondering were there  any pearls of wisdom that particularly  resonated and stay with you that you  like to share with us today  I think it's right to be idealistic I  think when people say you shouldn't be  idealistic they're really concerned  about their ability to be successful and  to be idealistic at the same time and I  think the two are totally consistent I  got into venture capital  because I thought it was socially good  Britain had three million unemployed and  I thought it was a good thing to do and  it created a lot of jobs and it improved  competitiveness of the country and it  improved the average standard of living  but they really didn't bridge the gaps  between rich and poor what we've  discovered is that two slogans of my  generation growth will cure economic  ills and the quality of opportunity  don't out to be empty slogans for those  whom society leaves behind economic  growth creates an even bigger gap  between rich and poor and the quality of  opportunity is an empty phrase for  somebody born into a family where both  parents are unemployed and have a drug  habit unless we begin to focus on  improving the quality of our society our  societies are going to break apart we  already see major divides occurring  within our cities within our countries  within within regions and I think the  zeitgeist today  unlike the zeitgeist in 1977 to 69 when  I was where you are sitting now which  was small is going to be beautiful and  innovation is going to come from  technology the zeitgeist today is being  impactful is going to be beautiful and  being impactful is going to change our  lives in the same way that technology  has done well on that positive note  please join me in thanking Sir Ronald
 

